    <string name="title_air_quality_so2">SO₂ - Sulphur dioxide</string>
    <string name="title_air_quality_no2">NO₂ - Nitrogen dioxide</string>
    <string name="title_air_quality_pm10">PM₁₀ - Particulate matter 10</string>
    <string name="title_air_quality_pm25">PM₂.₅ - Particulate matter 2.5</string>
    <string name="title_air_quality_o3">O₃ - Ozone</string>
    <string name="title_air_quality_co">CO - Carbon monoxide</string>

    <string name="content_air_quality_so2_information">Sulfur dioxide, or SO₂, is a gaseous pollutant primarily emitted from the combustion of fossil fuels such as coal and oil. It is responsible for the characteristic pungent odor when present in high concentrations. SO₂ can cause respiratory problems and contribute to the formation of acid rain.</string>
    <string name="content_air_quality_no2_information">Nitrogen dioxide, or NO₂, is a reddish-brown gas emitted by vehicles, power plants, and industrial facilities. It is an indicator of traffic-related air pollution. NO₂ can irritate the respiratory tract, worsen asthma symptoms, and contribute to the formation of smog.</string>
    <string name="content_air_quality_pm10_information">PM₁₀ refers to fine particulate matter suspended in the air, with a diameter of less than 10 micrometers. It can originate from natural sources like desert dust, as well as human activities such as fuel combustion, industrial emissions, and traffic. PM₁₀ can penetrate deep into the respiratory system and cause health problems such as irritation, allergies, and cardiovascular issues.</string>
    <string name="content_air_quality_pm25_information">PM₂.₅ refers to fine particulate matter suspended in the air, with a diameter of less than 2.5 micrometers. It is mainly produced by incomplete combustion of fossil fuels, industrial emissions, and traffic. PM₂.₅ is of particular concern as it can penetrate deep into the lungs and even enter the bloodstream, causing severe respiratory problems, cardiovascular diseases, and long-term adverse health effects.</string>
    <string name="content_air_quality_o3_information">Ozone, or O₃, is a gas present in the atmosphere that can be beneficial or harmful depending on its location. At ground level, ozone is a pollutant formed from chemical reactions between greenhouse gas emissions and sunlight. It can cause respiratory problems, irritate the respiratory tract, and worsen existing lung diseases. However, in the stratosphere, ozone plays an important role in protecting the Earth from ultraviolet radiation.</string>
    <string name="content_air_quality_co_information">Carbon monoxide, or CO, is a colorless and odorless gas produced by incomplete combustion of fossil fuels such as gasoline, coal, and wood. It is primarily emitted by vehicles, heating appliances, and generators. CO is dangerous as it binds to hemoglobin in the blood, reducing its capacity to transport oxygen. High exposure to CO can cause symptoms such as headaches, dizziness, nausea, and in severe cases, it can be fatal.</string>

    <string name="content_about_application_section">OpenWeather was born during the COVID lockdown, from a simple idea: learning Android development by building something useful. First written in Java, it has since moved to Kotlin and Jetpack Compose, and gained widgets and a whole new interface along the way.\n\nIt shows no ads and does not track you: the app is free software, under the GPLv3, and its code is open to anyone who wants to read it or contribute.</string>
